// Viṣṇu Sahasranāma — concepts 801–900 (English, own paraphrases).
// Titles are IAST nominatives in Śaṅkara-tradition numbering; summaries
// condense the Śaṅkara–Satyabhāṣyam readings in this project's own words.
// See vishnu-sahasranama-source-provenance.ts.
export const vsConcepts801900: any[] = [
  { id: "vs-nama-801", title: "Akṣobhyaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The unagitated, whom no wave can stir. Akṣobhya follows the golden drop (previous name): a point of gold calming oceans, stillness condensed to a bindu that settles every swell. The tradition reads unagitatedness as sovereignty rather than indifference — depths unmoved because depths contain all motion. Agitation belongs to surfaces; He is the lake beneath surfaces. Settle one agitation today into a golden point of attention. (Stotra verse 86.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-086"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-802", title: "Sarvavāgīśvareśvaraḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "Lord of the lords of speech. Every tongue has a master — grammarians, poets, gods of eloquence — and He masters the masters, ruling each one's ruler as the Gītā's Kṛṣṇa calls Himself the syllable among words. Speech here is cosmic administration: Veda, mantra, command all report upward to one throne. The verse stations this lordship beside unagitated calm, so eloquence reads as governed, not garrulous. Place one sentence today under His censorship before speaking it. (Stotra verse 86.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-086"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-803", title: "Mahāhradaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The great lake, deep with joy's waters. Hrada is still water held without banks of effort: delight hectolitres deep, coolness that needs no replenishing. After speech's lordship, vast inward water answers outward utterance — the eloquent Lord is also the silent reservoir. Lakes give without emptying because they receive without grasping; so does He. Draw one cup of calm from depth rather than piping it from agitation. (Stotra verse 86.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-086"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-804", title: "Mahāgartaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The great depth-chasm, the hollow that hallows. Garta is the pit, the abyss, the low place all waters seek — and greatness here descends rather than towers, depth glorified instead of height. The hymn dares to praise hollowness: the chasm that receives the fallen, the lowliness that funds the lake above it. Valleys collect what peaks shed. Descend once willingly into a low place and find Him already there. (Stotra verse 86.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-086"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-805", title: "Mahābhūtaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The great being, the mighty element. Mahābhūta gathers the five gross elements into their source-element: earth, water, fire, air and ether as His five fingers, and Himself the palm. Greatness here is ontological mass — existence extra, being big — the stuff of the world recognized as a Person. After lake and chasm, being itself is praised as element, not abstraction. Touch one solid thing today as His great body. (Stotra verse 86.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-086"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-806", title: "Mahānidhiḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The great treasure, in whom all wealth is saved. Nidhi is the buried hoard, the reserve fund of the cosmos: fortunes filed, riches reserved against every famine of merit. The verse closes its septet by banking greatness — lake, depth, being, all deposited as treasure. Saving here is divine thrift: nothing good is ever lost, every offering accrues. Deposit one unseen good deed where only He audits. (Stotra verse 86.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-086"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-807", title: "Kumudaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "Who makes the earth happy, lotus-joy. Kumuda is the night-lotus blooming for the moon, delight earthed and terra-firmed: gladness grounded, glee given soil. The new verse turns from stored treasure to flowering — wealth kept becomes joy spread, the hoard opening as petals. Earth Samṛddhi herself is His beloved's field, and He gladdens ground before gracing minds. Gladden one patch of ground, literal or human, today. (Stotra verse 87.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-087"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-808", title: "Kundaraḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The earth-cleaver, who notes the results of good deeds. Kundara joins the plough's violence with the ledger's exactness: cleaving ground for sowing while marking every merit sown, furrows that are also accounts. Cleaving here is kindly — hardness broken so tenderness can root, the field wounded into fertility. Merit is never mislaid because the Cleaver keeps the roll. Break one hardness kindly so something can grow in it. (Stotra verse 87.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-087"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-809", title: "Kundaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "Pure as jasmine, attractive in whiteness. Kunda is the white blossom of untarnished innocence: purity petalled, brightness infloresced, the stainless made fragrant. After cleaving comes flowering — the clod's wound answered by a white bloom, labour's brown yielding to worship's white. Whiteness wins because it accuses nothing; it simply outshines. Wear one unstained motive today like a jasmine behind the ear. (Stotra verse 87.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-087"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-810", title: "Parjanyaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The rain-cloud, scheduled shower of blessings. Parjanya is the Vedic cloud-god absorbed into Viṣṇu: grace graduated, precipitation appointed, the sky keeping its calendar of mercy as the Gītā's Kṛṣṇa sends rain that feeds sacrifice that feeds rain. Jasmine (previous name) needs watering, and here comes the watering — beauty sustained by weather, not by effort. The flower does not pump the cloud. Receive one grace as rain, not wages. (Stotra verse 87.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-087"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-811", title: "Pāvanaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The ever-purifying (named again). Pāvana is cleansing chronic, washings without end: purity perpetuated, the laundry of the world never closing. Repetition is doctrine — purification cannot be done once, because living soils daily, so the Purifier stays on duty. Wind and water (the neighbouring names) are His two detergents; remembrance is the third. Wash one soiled corner of conduct today without despairing of rewashing tomorrow. (Stotra verse 87.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-087"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-812", title: "Anilaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The unslipping wind (named again). Anila is air that never errs, breath blameless: the atmosphere unerring in its rounds, respiration righteous in every creature. The tradition hears an-ila as motion without slipping — the wind that never misses its errand, carrying scent, seed and prayer exactly where due. After the cloud comes its driver; rain arrives because wind obeys. Breathe once consciously as His unerring errand. (Stotra verse 87.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-087"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-813", title: "Amṛtāśaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The nectar-rayed, who drinks and sheds deathlessness. Amṛtāṃśa — moon-rayed, nectar-portioned — makes immortality luminous: drafts undying served as moonlight, the night sky pouring soma for tired eyes. The verse pairs ray with body (next name): light first, then flesh — immortality both seen and worn. Moonlight asks nothing and revives everything; so does His glance. Sit once in moonlight, literal or inward, and sip. (Stotra verse 87.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-087"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-814", title: "Amṛtavapuḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The nectar-bodied, immortal in frame. Amṛtavapus completes the previous ray: undying light now unfading flesh, form unfailing, the deathless tailored into a body that can be embraced. The tradition insists immortality is not formlessness — the Lord's limbs are themselves ambrosia, and contact with any one confers life. Ray promises; body delivers. Honour one body, yours or another's, as capable of carrying nectar. (Stotra verse 87.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-087"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-815", title: "Sarvajñaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The omniscient (named again). Sarvajña here re-knows after nectar: lore re-learned, wisdom wise about tenderness — knowledge that has passed through moonlight and therefore does not scorch. Repetition places omniscience among gentlenesses (flower, rain, nectar) so that all-knowing reads as all-tending. He knows the wound and the remedy together, diagnosis wedded to cure. Confess one thing to the All-knowing instead of hiding it from the half-knowing. (Stotra verse 87.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-087"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-816", title: "Sarvatomukhaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "Faced on every side. Sarvatomukha turns surveillance into welcome: regards around, looks legion — no direction unmet, no approach unanswered, the Gītā's universal form with a face for every fugitive. The verse closes its arc from earth-joy to all-faces: what began in soil ends in omnipresence, the field's God facing every field-worker. Fleeing is futile because every exit has His face — which is mercy, not trap. Face one neglected direction of your life today. (Stotra verse 87.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-087"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-817", title: "Sulabhaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The easily won, attained without tariff. Sulabha startles after cosmic faces: infinity on easy terms, the All-faced turning out to be near — approachability assured, nearness natural. Śaṅkara's lineage stresses that ease is His nature, not His mood: the door stands open because openness is the architecture. Difficulty lies in our detours, never in His distance. Attempt one approach today without preparing worthiness first. (Stotra verse 88.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-088"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-818", title: "Suvrataḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "Keeper of fair vows (named again). Suvrata validates pledges: vows accepted, promises processed — the Easily-won proving reliable, accessibility_answerable. The tradition pairs His vow-keeping with the devotee's vow-making: He keeps His side (protection) so strictly that our side becomes possible. Where sulabha invites beginning, suvrata guarantees continuance. Keep one small vow exactly today, borrowing His exactness. (Stotra verse 88.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-088"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-819", title: "Siddhaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The Perfect, accomplishment itself (named again). Siddha is finish re-finished, done re-done: perfection not as polish but as completeness needing no supplement. The verse stations perfection between vows and warfare — vow-keeping ripened into completeness before battle is joined, so that foes are met by fullness, not by effort. He fights from perfection, never toward it. Complete one unfinished trifle today in His manner. (Stotra verse 88.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-088"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-820", title: "Śatrujit", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "Ever victorious over foes. Śatrujit ends enmity by outlasting it: hostiles halted, haters humbled — not humiliated but quieted, opposition stilled rather than slaughtered (slaughter comes next). Victory here is constitutional: He cannot lose because there is no field outside Him on which losing could occur. Gentleness to friends (previous names) shows its other face. Surrender one inner foe — resentment, envy — to His certain victory. (Stotra verse 88.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-088"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-821", title: "Śatrutāpanaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The scorcher of enemies. Śatrutāpana heats hostiles: foes fired, haters held to heaven's flame — victory (previous name) now applied as temperature, opposition withered by radiance rather than crushed by weight. The tradition distinguishes the two: jit conquers the foe's strength, tāpana consumes the foe's will, leaving the person salvageable. Fire refines what force would merely break. Offer one hostility to be scorched, not nursed. (Stotra verse 88.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-088"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-822", title: "Nyagrodhaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The banyan above all beings. Nyagrodha spreads shelter that roots downward from its own branches: refuge sprawling, protection self-propagating — the tree that plants its own pillars, like grace establishing new supports wherever shade is needed. After warfare comes shade: victory's purpose is rest for the defended. Villagers have always known what theologians prove — shade is doctrine. Rest one weary person in your shade today. (Stotra verse 88.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-088"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-823", title: "Udumbaraḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The fig, nourisher with hidden flowers. Udumbara feeds without display: fruit furnishing, food forthcoming from a tree whose blossoms stay inward — bounty modest, generosity unadvertised. The Upaniṣads seat gods in its wood; village and Veda agree that shade must also feed. Banyan (previous name) shelters; fig sustains — protection completed by provision. Feed one hunger quietly, without exhibiting the blossom. (Stotra verse 88.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-088"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-824", title: "Aśvatthaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The pipal, the impermanent-yet-eternal tree. Aśvattha sheds leaves daily yet never dies: flux founded, roots deathless — the Gītā's inverted tree (XV.1) with roots above and branches below, which Kṛṣṇa says must be cut by non-attachment yet here is praised as Himself. Time's very transience, owned and hallowed. Leaves fall; the tree abides; both are Him. Release one falling leaf of a habit without mourning the tree. (Stotra verse 88.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-088"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-825", title: "Cāṇūrāndhraniṣūdanaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "Slayer of the wrestler Cāṇūra. The boy Kṛṣṇa, challenged in Kaṃsa's arena, pinned muscle with miracle: tyranny thrown, arena pride pinned — play killing power, festival overturning empire. The verse closes its trees-and-war arc with remembered history: shade (banyan), food (fig), cosmos (pipal), then the datable deed proving metaphysics walks in Mathurā. Tyranny always arranges arenas; He always accepts as the smaller wrestler. Wrestle one petty tyranny playfully. (Stotra verse 88.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-088"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-826", title: "Sahasrārciḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The thousand-rayed. Sahasrārci opens the fire-septet: beams by battalions, light legion, shine soldiered — radiance as army, dawn as muster. A thousand rays means no shadow survives and no corner is favoured; impartiality expressed as intensity. After the arena comes the sunrise over it — history's dust lit from every side. Turn one shadowed corner of conduct toward a single ray. (Stotra verse 89.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-089"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-827", title: "Saptajihvaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The seven-tongued, as sacrificial fire. Saptajihva tastes oblations sevenfold: flames fluent, tongues licking ghee in seven colours — Agni's equipage absorbed into Viṣṇu, ritual fire recognized as His appetite. Seven tongues mean no offering is refused for want of a mouth; every gift finds its flame. The thousand rays (previous name) now focus into mouths: light learning to eat. Offer one small act as ghee to a single tongue. (Stotra verse 89.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-089"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-828", title: "Saptaidhāḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The seven-flamed, kindled sevenfold. Saptaidhas braids burns: blazes bundled, seven fuels flaring as one conflagration — the same fire seen in its seven modes, variety inside unity. Tongues (previous name) taste; flames display: appetite made visible, hunger turned to illumination. Worship's physics is honest — fire both consumes and shows. Kindle one of your seven energies (senses, breath, mind) deliberately today. (Stotra verse 89.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-089"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-829", title: "Saptavāhanaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The seven-horsed, driving the sun's car. Saptavāhana harnesses seven steeds — days driven, dawns drawn across the sky in Vedic relay, time itself as chariot-team. Flames (previous name) now travel: fire given horses, sacrifice given schedule, so that radiance arrives daily, not randomly. Regularity is mercy's vehicle. Greet one dawn as His chariot passing your street. (Stotra verse 89.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-089"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-830", title: "Amūrtiḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The formless, beyond all figure. Amūrti follows fire's full equipage and cancels it: figure fled finally, form forgone — ritual's blaze opening onto mystery, the God who rode seven horses now outrunning every shape. The tradition is precise: formlessness is not absence but excess, too much reality for outlines. Fire prepared the eyes; formlessness rewards them by exceeding sight. Release one mental image of God without losing God. (Stotra verse 89.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-089"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-831", title: "Anaghaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The sinless (named again). Anagha re-stainlesses after formlessness: fault re-faultlessed — purity reaffirmed precisely where mystery might excuse laxity. The formless is not the vague, and the inconceivable (next name) is not the unaccountable; stainlessness anchors transcendence in ethics. Sinlessness here is incapacity, not achievement: He cannot stain because He has no surface for stain. Borrow one stainless hour today. (Stotra verse 89.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-089"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-832", title: "Acintyaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The inconceivable. Acintya caps concepts thankfully: thought thwarted, mind mothballed — not insulted but rested, the intellect honourably discharged after carrying the climber to the cliff's edge. Fire, formlessness and sinlessness converge here: all that was kindled, cancelled and cleansed now confesses limit. Inconceivability is hospitality — room left for wonder inside doctrine. End one argument with wonder instead of victory. (Stotra verse 89.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-089"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-833", title: "Bhayakṛt", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The fear-maker for the wicked. Bhayakṛt deals dread deservedly: terrors targeted, fears focused — the Inconceivable showing teeth to those who mistake mercy for vacancy. The tradition insists this is compassion in armour: fear is the first tutor of the untutorable, and His terror is calibrated medicine, never malice. One energy wears two faces (next name completes the pair). Let one guilty fear do its tutoring work instead of anaesthetizing it. (Stotra verse 89.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-089"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-834", title: "Bhayanāśanaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The destroyer of the devotee's fear. Bhayanāśana dissolves dreads: terrors terminated, frights finished — the same energy that made fear (previous name) now unmakes it, one power with two addresses, medicine and surgery from one hand. The verse's arc from thousand rays to this dissolving proves fire's purpose: light that first exposes (fear made) then enfolds (fear destroyed). Sleep, nightmare, death — all covered. Hand over one specific fear to be destroyed tonight. (Stotra verse 89.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-089"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-835", title: "Aṇuḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The atomic, subtler than the subtle. Aṇu opens the famous dozen contraries: small supreme, littleness loftiest, the mote mightiest — the Upaniṣadic aṇor aṇīyān (smaller than the smallest) claimed as personal name. Minuteness here is not insignificance but inaccessibility to coarse grasp; the small escapes the net that catches the big. Vastness follows immediately, so smallness is never belittlement. Honour one tiny thing — a seed, a minute, a kindness — as His scale. (Stotra verse 90.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-090"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-836", title: "Bṛhat", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The vast, exceeding every measure. Bṛhat answers the atom with boundlessness: vastness valid, hugeness holy — bṛhat is the root of Brahman itself, growth without глянец, greatness as being's natural size. The pair (atomic yet vast) states non-duality as paradox, not proposition: He does not compromise between scales but occupies both fully. Bigness that excludes nothing cannot threaten anything. Let one vastness — sky, ocean, grief — hold you instead of frightening you. (Stotra verse 90.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-090"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-837", title: "Kṛśaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The lean, the slender. Kṛśa praises holy thinness: leanness luminous, spareness splendid — the worn frame of the ascetic, the remnant after deluge, fasting made metaphysical. After atomic-and-vast comes lean-and-gross: the scale-paradox descends from cosmos to body, and thinness is hallowed lest only bulk be worshipped. The tradition loves this name's democracy — God in the meagre, not only the massive. Honour one lean season of your life as full of Him. (Stotra verse 90.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-090"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-838", title: "Sthūlaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The gross, the stout. Sthūla blesses bulk: mass magnified, weight worshipped — the elephant, the mountain, the heavy-bodied devotee all included, lest leanness (previous name) become a new snobbery. Grossness here is generosity made dimensional: roominess, abundance, the unapologetic much. The pair holds fasting and feasting in one verse as one body. Stop apologizing for one honest bulk — of body, of appetite, of joy. (Stotra verse 90.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-090"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-839", title: "Guṇabhṛt", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The bearer of qualities. Guṇabhṛt shoulders the strands: attributes assumed, modes maintained — sattva, rajas, tamas carried as His ropes, not suffered as His bonds. Bearing is mastery: He wears qualities the way a king wears robes, removable, serviceable, never confused with the wearer. This is the saguṇa charter — God with attributes, approachable, describable. Thank Him for consenting to have qualities you can love. (Stotra verse 90.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-090"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-840", title: "Nirguṇaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The qualityless. Nirguṇa sheds the strands: attributes absented, modes mortified — the same Lord, robes laid aside, standing in the nirguṇa truth the Upaniṣads praise as not-this, not-this. The verse refuses to choose: bearer and bereft side by side, saguṇa and nirguṇa as two true sentences, not two gods. Qualities are His condescension; qualitylessness His nature; both are mercy. Rest once in attributelessness without images. (Stotra verse 90.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-090"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-841", title: "Mahān", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The Great. Mahān gathers the contraries into sheer magnitude: greatness grand, largeness lordly — mahat of Sāṅkhya's first evolute and the Mahānārāyaṇa's acclamation in one syllable. After bearer-and-bereft, greatness is the synthesis that needs no synthesis: big enough for both predicates, exceeding every either-or. Greatness here is not comparison (greater than) but absoluteness (great, period). Stand once, briefly, before sheer Great without measuring. (Stotra verse 90.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-090"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-842", title: "Adhṛtaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The unsupported, carried by nothing. Adhṛta declines all propping: support spurned, bearing bypassed — the roof with no pillars, the arch with no abutment, asking metaphysics' hardest question (what holds the Holder?) and answering: nothing needs to. Dependence is a creaturely condition; He exhibits independence so creatures may borrow courage. The pair with self-supported (next) completes the edifice: unheld, yet holding Himself. Lean once on the Unsupported. (Stotra verse 90.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-090"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-843", title: "Svadhṛtaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The self-supported. Svadhṛta answers the unsupported (previous name): bearing by being, support from Self — sustenance same-sourced, the serpent of eternity holding its own tail, the Gītā's claim of establishing all by a portion of Himself. Unheld from without, upheld from within: aseity in one compound. What holds Him is what He is. Practise one act of inward self-support today. (Stotra verse 90.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-090"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-844", title: "Svāsyaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The fair-faced, whose mouth voiced the Vedas. Svāsya keeps paradox beautiful: features fine, mouth musical — after atoms, voids, supports and negations, a Face, lest metaphysics forget devotion. The tradition hears su-āsya as both lovely countenance and gracious mouth: beauty that speaks blessing, the Vedas themselves as His speech. Abstraction never abolishes affection here. Picture one gracious expression of His today and answer it with your own face. (Stotra verse 90.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-090"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-845", title: "Prāgvaṃśaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The first of the lineage. Prāgvaṃśa stands at the pedigree's head: ancestry apex, forebears first — the eldest of every house, before whom all gotras begin and after whom none can claim seniority. After metaphysical contraries comes tradition's root: paradox has a family, and He fathers it. Lineage here is continuity's guarantee — wisdom handed, not improvised. Honour one elder of your own lineage as His representative. (Stotra verse 90.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-090"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-846", title: "Vaṃśavardhanaḥ", category: "Hard-Radiant", summary: "The increaser of the lineage. Vaṃśavardhana lengthens lines: houses heightened, clans continued — the First (previous name) proving fertile, tradition not merely founded but flourishing, every worthy heir a fresh evidence. The verse closes its dozen contraries in fruitfulness: paradox does not sterilize, it propagates. Dynasties of disciples are His doing. Foster one junior — in family, craft or faith — as His increase. (Stotra verse 90.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-090"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-847", title: "Bhārabhṛt", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The bearer of the universe's burden. Bhārabhṛt carries weight before granting wishes (next arc): burden borne broadly — Atlas without strain, the tortoise and boar and charioteer in one porter. Service precedes largesse throughout this verse: He lifts before He lavishes, shoulders before He showers. The cosmos is heavy; His shoulder is permanent. Transfer one carried weight to Him in so many words today. (Stotra verse 91.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-091"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-848", title: "Kathitaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The narrated, glorified in all scriptures. Kathita is God as story: told through texts, narrated notably — the Lord become tellable, history's most repeated Subject, from Veda to Purāṇa to grandmother's tale. After burden-bearing comes fame: service storied, labour legended, so that carriers learn endurance from His precedent. He lets Himself be talked about — condescension of celebrity. Retell one of His deeds accurately today. (Stotra verse 91.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-091"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-849", title: "Yogī", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The realized through yoga, union's practitioner. Yogin names Him the supreme practitioner: joined genuinely, yoked yieldingly — meditation's master who needs no practice yet exhibits it, establishing the discipline by example as Kṛṣṇa establishes dharma. The Narrated (previous name) is not only talked about but practised toward; story becomes sādhanā. Practise one joining — breath with body, mind with act — in His company. (Stotra verse 91.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-091"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-850", title: "Yogīśaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The lord of yogīs, greatest among the yoked. Yogīśa out-yogīs the yogīs: joiners joined best — every samādhi a suburb of His consciousness, every adept's lamp lit from His grid. Where yogī (previous name) practises alongside, yogīśa presides above: example crowned by eminence, fellowship by sovereignty. The hierarchy humbles without discouraging — there is always a further Yogi to follow. Follow one instruction of the tradition exactly. (Stotra verse 91.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-091"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-851", title: "Sarvakāmadaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The granter of every honest wish. Sarvakāmada warrants wants wholly: wishes worked, desires done — burden-bearing (verse's opening) ripening into bounty, service flowering as largesse. The tradition adds the honest qualifier: He grants all desires by purifying them in the granting, giving the toy with the lesson inside. Wishing is itself a prayer He answers pedagogically. Present one desire to Him unedited and watch it schooled. (Stotra verse 91.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-091"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-852", title: "Āśramaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The hermitage, rest for wanderers. Āśrama is retreat readied: asylums arranged — the Lord as lodging, the fourth stage's forest-hut writ cosmic, where the road-footsore unlace their burdens (first name's burdens arrive here). After wish-granting comes rest: desire's fatigue answered by shelter, having by abiding. Monasteries are His branch offices. Provide one resting-place — room, hour, silence — for a wanderer. (Stotra verse 91.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-091"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-853", title: "Śramaṇaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The ascetic, whose austerity humbles the heedless. Śramaṇa toils teaching: effort edifying, labour lecturing — the Hermitage (previous name) inhabited by an Exemplar, rest earned by strenuousness, stillness underwritten by tapas. Fame with fasting (the verse's theme): narrated glory practised as bodily discipline. His thinness (next name) is this name's signature. Undertake one small deliberate austerity today. (Stotra verse 91.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-091"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-854", title: "Kṣāmaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The thin, worn slender yet surviving (named again). Kṣāma is slenderness surviving: remnant remaining — the essence enduring when bulk dissolves, as after the deluge only the seed barge floats. Repetition places thinness in the wish-and-rest verse: bounty does not pamper, shelter does not fatten; the Ascetic (previous name) stays lean amid largesse. Leanness is readiness. Reduce one bulk — of meal, of chatter, of baggage — today. (Stotra verse 91.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-091"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-855", title: "Suparṇaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The fair-winged (named again). Suparṇa re-feathers flight: wings re-winged — Garuḍa's plumage, Veda's metres as pinions, the beautiful lift that carries Viṣṇu Himself yet belongs to Viṣṇu. After thinness comes flight: leanness proved aerodynamic, renunciation airborne. The fair-winged is thinness transfigured — what was worn slender now soars. Beautiful wings grow from disciplined hollows. Lift one low thought to a higher altitude. (Stotra verse 91.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-091"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-856", title: "Vāyuvāhanaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The wind-borne, riding and ruling the winds (named again). Vāyuvāhana re-administers airs: breezes re-bossed — Garuḍa's flight (previous name) given its element, wings given weather, the Lord both carried by wind and commanding it. Vehicle and driver coincide: He rides what He rules, travels by His own ordinance. Stillness (hermitage) and speed (wind) reconcile in one Person. Let one inward wind — restlessness, inspiration — carry you to Him today. (Stotra verse 91.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-091"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-857", title: "Dhanurdharaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The bow-bearer, Rāma with strung Śārṅga. Dhanurdhara draws aim accurate: archery apt, quiver quick — resolve weaponized, gentleness given a bowstring, the prince's weapon proving protection has range. The new verse shifts from wind to arms: flight's freedom guarded by force, the hermitage fenced by the bow. His bow never trembles because His purpose never wavers. Aim one resolve and loose it cleanly. (Stotra verse 92.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-092"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-858", title: "Dhanurvedaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "Knower of archery's science. Dhanurveda schools strings: shafts studied, bow-lore learned — force educated, violence graduated into discipline, the warrior's manual mastered by its divine Author. Bearing the bow (previous name) is practice; knowing its Veda is principle — deed backed by doctrine, as deeds-become-doctrine elsewhere in this hymn. Study one craft of yours to its principles, not merely its tricks. (Stotra verse 92.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-092"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-859", title: "Daṇḍaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The rod of justice. Daṇḍa is punishment principled: chastisement chaste, correction clean — the king's staff, the judge's sentence, absorbed into God's hand so that penalty reads as pedagogy, never vengeance. Bow (range) narrows to rod (closeness): distant aim and intimate correction in one armoury. The rod that schools is the bow unstrung for teaching. Accept one correction as His rod, not your humiliation. (Stotra verse 92.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-092"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-860", title: "Damayitā", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The subduer of the lawless. Damayitā reins rebellion: wildness warded, unruliness undone — the Rod (previous name) wielded to tame, not to break, wild horses gentled for the chariot, not slaughtered. Subduing ripens into self-mastery (next name): His taming teaches taming, force educated into restraint. Every subdued rebel is a future citizen of His order. Subdue one wild impulse by reining, not by killing it. (Stotra verse 92.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-092"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-861", title: "Damaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "Self-mastery itself (named again). Dama re-masters control: mastery re-mastered — the Subduer (previous name) revealed as self-subdued, force's education completing in the Educator Himself. Restraint is not His tactic but His nature: the bow steady because the hand is still, the hand still because the Self is settled. Discipline in us is likeness to Him. Master one impulse today as portraiture of God. (Stotra verse 92.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-092"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-862", title: "Aparājitaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The undefeated (named again). Aparājita re-declines defeat: losing re-lost never — self-mastery (previous name) proved invincible, the steady hand making the steady victory. Repetition in the arms-verse teaches that force educated into restraint cannot be beaten, because nothing outside can outbid stillness. Undefeatedness is not stubbornness but establishment: standing where no wind reaches. Stand one ground of principle without counterattacking. (Stotra verse 92.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-092"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-863", title: "Sarvasahaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The all-enduring, able in every work. Sarvasaha bears all: endurance entire, patience pandemic in the old good sense — every blow absorbed, every labour undertaken, the Undefeated (previous name) proving invulnerability by stamina rather than armour. Endurance here ordains (next name): what survives everything earns the right to assign everything. Outlast one irritation today instead of outarguing it. (Stotra verse 92.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-092"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-864", title: "Niyantā", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The ordainer, appointing each to duty. Niyantā staffs stations: posts posted, charges charged — all-endurance (previous name) promoted to administration, the Survivor assigning posts because only the Tried knows placements. Law's source stands above law (next names): ordaining is personal wisdom, not mechanical rule. Every duty is His appointment letter. Execute one assigned duty as His direct posting. (Stotra verse 92.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-092"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-865", title: "Aniyamaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The rule-free, bound by no regulation. Aniyama transcends codes: law transcended, regulation risen above — the Ordainer (previous name) exceeding His own ordinances, as the Gītā's Lord acts without obligation though sustaining all obligation. (Some recensions read niyamaḥ, the very embodiment of rule; both readings confess law's source.) Freedom above law guarantees law is service, not tyranny. Obey one rule freely today, as He transcends freely. (Stotra verse 92.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-092"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-866", title: "Ayamaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The death-free, beyond restraint and ending. Ayama defies demise: limits left, ending exceeded — (some recensions read yamaḥ, the Restrainer or Death Himself; either way, death's master, not its subject). The arms-verse closes beyond both law and death: rule-free (previous name) and end-free, force fully educated into freedom. What cannot be bound cannot be buried. Release one death-anxiety into His deathlessness. (Stotra verse 92.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-092"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-867", title: "Sattvavān", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "Full of being and strength. Sattvavān is substance strong: being brawny, essence energetic — sattva as both existence and luminous goodness, the real that is also the good. The new verse ascends being-clarity-veracity: ontology first, then lucidity, then truth — strength as the groundfloor of sanctity. He is strong the way light is strong: by nature, not by effort. Ground one good act in sheer being rather than in striving. (Stotra verse 93.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-093"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-868", title: "Sāttvikaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The abider in luminous sattva. Sāttvika camps clarity: lucidity lodged, goodness garrisoned — strength (previous name) settling into transparency, power clarifying into purity. Abiding matters: sattva is not His visit but His residence, the guna He wears when He would be seen truly. Rajas acts and tamas rests, but sattva reveals — and He dwells in revelation. Clarify one motive today until it turns transparent. (Stotra verse 93.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-093"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-869", title: "Satyaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "Truth Himself (named again). Satya re-truths the ascent: verity re-verified — being (strength) clarified (purity) culminating in personhood, honesty as likeness to Him, every exact word a small incarnation, as the Upaniṣads call truth Brahman's face. Repetition through the hymn re-anchors each zone: whatever else He is, He is this. Speak one precise truth without embroidery today. (Stotra verse 93.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-093"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-870", title: "Satyadharmaparāyaṇaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "Devoted to the dharma of truth. Satyadharmaparāyaṇa lodges law as life-goal: rightness resided, truth-legislated — truth (previous name) extended into conduct, veracity grown into jurisprudence, the Person becoming the Path. Devotion language startles: the Supreme devoted to something — to truth-dharma — proving excellence serves order rather than owning it. Devote one conduct to truth's dharma, not to advantage. (Stotra verse 93.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-093"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-871", title: "Abhiprāyaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The Intention, dwelling behind seeking hearts. Abhiprāya claims purpose as His residence: motives monitored mercifully — even our aims are His address, intention itself a shrine He inhabits. After truth-dharma comes inwardness: law's keeper moves inside the will, reading drafts before they become deeds. Nothing is merely meant; everything meant is witnessed. Examine one intention today as though traced over His shoulder. (Stotra verse 93.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-093"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-872", title: "Priyārhaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "Worthy of the dearest offerings. Priyārha merits love: affection earned, dearness deserved — intention (previous name) proving lovable, the inward God attracting inward treasure. Worthiness here invites, never invoices: He deserves the dearest, yet accepts the wild leaf and sip of water the Gītā promises. Value is magnet, not toll. Offer one dear thing — time, pride, preference — grudgelessly. (Stotra verse 93.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-093"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-873", title: "Arhaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The Worthy, worthiest of all worship. Arha ripens reverence: honour highest — priyārha's dearest-worthiness universalized into worship's sole proper object, the Vedic arha (deserving) standing absolute. Worthiness is His claim and our compass: adoration finds its address, praise its proportion. All altars are attempts at this one. Direct one worship — song, silence, service — to the Worthiest. (Stotra verse 93.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-093"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-874", title: "Priyakṛt", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The doer of loving acts. Priyakṛt labours love: affection acted, kindness kept — worship's Worthy (previous name) answering worship with works, adoration reciprocated in deeds. The tradition delights in the exchange: devotees do the dear for Him, He does the dear for them, affection's commerce with profit both ways. Love here is verb before noun. Do one concrete loving act today without announcing it. (Stotra verse 93.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-093"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-875", title: "Prītivardhanaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The increaser of joy. Prītivardhana grows gladness: delight deepened, joy justified — loving acts (previous name) compounded into increasing happiness, affection's arithmetic proving love is the one fund that grows by spending. The verse closes its ascent (being, clarity, truth, intention, love) in economics of bliss: all prior names pay dividends here. Increase one person's joy deliberately today. (Stotra verse 93.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-093"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-876", title: "Vihāyasagatiḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "Whose path is the sky. Vihāyasagati highways heaven: firmament footed, welkin walked — the joy-increaser (previous verse's close) now striding open blue, bliss given altitude. Sky-going means unobstructed: no tolls, no ruts, the Lord's traffic never congested. Birds and winds are His fellow-travellers; so is the upward mind. Walk one stretch — of road, of thought — skyward today. (Stotra verse 94.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-094"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-877", title: "Jyotiḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "Light itself, of all lights the source. Jyotis is brightness best: lustre loftiest, shine supreme — the Muṇḍaka's light of lights, by whose shining all shines, claimed here as personal name. Sky-going (previous name) now blazes: the path is paved with radiance, travel and illumination one. Darkness is not His opposite but His absentee. Carry one light — lamp, word, explanation — into a dark place. (Stotra verse 94.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-094"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-878", title: "Suruciḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The beautifully radiant, whose will lights worlds. Suruci polishes purpose: volition vivid — light (previous name) given intention, radiance with a will behind it, beauty that means something. The tradition hears su-ruci as fair-delight: His shining pleases as well as illumines, aesthetics wedded to ethics. Light that merely exposes blinds; willed radiance guides. Illuminate one decision beautifully rather than harshly. (Stotra verse 94.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-094"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-879", title: "Hutabhuk", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The eater of oblations. Hutabhuk consumes earth's gifts: offerings operating inward — gifts gorged godly, heaven eating what earth surrenders, Agni's mouth recognized as His. Sky-going light (previous names) descends to dine: transcendence with appetite, glory that accepts lunch. Eating here is honouring — the offered is completed, not destroyed, by being consumed. Offer one act, one meal, one hour as oblation today. (Stotra verse 94.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-094"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-880", title: "Vibhuḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The pervader (named again). Vibhu re-forms manifoldly: shapes re-shaped — solar blaze (this verse's theme) prevented from localizing, lest light be mistaken for a lamp only. Repetition stations pervasion among sun-names deliberately: the sun is His eye, not His enclosure; radiance is His idiom, not His boundary. He fills what He fires. Feel one sunbeam as pervasion, not merely warmth. (Stotra verse 94.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-094"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-881", title: "Raviḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The sun who draws up essences. Ravi sips saps solarly: juices juiced, essences extracted — the drawer-up (rasānām ādāna) whose rays lift waters to clouds and vigour to veins, taxation as blessing, withdrawal as irrigation. Oblation-eating (previous name) reverses here: He who consumes also extracts, dinner and harvest in one deity. What He takes returns as rain. Let one extraction in your life — tax, loss, parting — promise rain. (Stotra verse 94.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-094"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-882", title: "Virocanaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The shining forth in countless ways. Virocana branches beams: rays ramified, glows grown — shining as display, the sun's self-exhibition, glory that enjoys being seen. After drawing up (previous name) comes showing forth: storing followed by revelation, the hidden yielding to splendour. The tradition distinguishes light (jyotis) from shining-forth (virocana): being versus appearing, both His. Appear once radiantly instead of hiding your light. (Stotra verse 94.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-094"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-883", title: "Sūryaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The sun, brilliance's source. Sūrya manufactures mornings: dawns delivered daily — daybreak as fidelity, the Eastern promise never defaulted, time's most reliable sacrament. Among six sun-names this is the classic: the orb itself, round, punctual, undeniable, worshipped from Gāyatrī to temple tower. Regularity is theology: He rises whether praised or not. Greet one sunrise — seen or remembered — as His reliability. (Stotra verse 94.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-094"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-884", title: "Savitā", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The impeller, begetter bringing forth worlds. Savitṛ egresses existences: beings born — the Gāyatrī's God impelling intellects as the sun impels growth, stimulation sacred, arousal hallowed. Sun (previous name) rises; Impeller acts: orb becoming agent, light becoming command. Every awakening — of seed, of mind, of conscience — is His impelling. Begin one begetting kindly: start a work, wake a talent, impel a good. (Stotra verse 94.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-094"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-885", title: "Ravilocanaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The sun-eyed. Ravilocana sees solarly: sight sun-powered, regard radiant — the eye that is itself a sun, illumination and inspection one organ. The verse closes its solar theology in vision: sky, light, will, eating, pervading, drawing, shining, orb, impelling — all gathered into a glance. His seeing is His shining; to be beheld by Him is to be lit. Meet one gaze today — human or solar — as His eye. (Stotra verse 94.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-094"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-886", title: "Anantaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The Endless (named again). Ananta re-infinites after the suns: boundlessness re-boundlessly — eternity practising beginnings (the verse's theme), the Serpent-couch's thousand hoods recalled, limitlessness that can still be addressed. Repetition here teaches that solar immensities do not exhaust Him; suns are episodes in endlessness. Every ending is His ellipsis. Trust one unfinished story of yours to the Endless. (Stotra verse 95.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-095"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-887", title: "Hutabhuk", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The oblation-eater (named again). Hutabhuk re-offers inwardly: gifts re-gorged godly — eating repeated in the endlessness verse so that consumption reads as eternal function, not occasional hunger. Sacrifice never ends because appetite never ends: the cosmos perpetually dining with its Lord, earth cooking and heaven tasting. Grudges excepted — those He declines. Re-offer one daily act as oblation. (Stotra verse 95.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-095"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-888", title: "Bhoktā", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The Enjoyer (named again). Bhoktṛ re-relishes: taste re-tasted — eating (previous name) ripening into enjoyment, consumption into connoisseurship, the Gītā's field-knower tasting prakṛti's flavours without being stained by them. Enjoyment here is sovereign: He savours without clutching, relishes without addicting. Pleasure is sacrament when the Enjoyer is remembered. Taste one pleasure thankfully instead of guiltily or greedily. (Stotra verse 95.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-095"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-889", title: "Sukhadaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The giver of joy (named again). Sukhada re-gives gladness: delight re-dealt — enjoying (previous name) transmuted into bestowing, consumption turned to blessing, the Enjoyer proving generous. The verse's alchemy runs eating-enjoying-giving: intake, relish, largesse — metabolism as mercy. Joy received is joy's seed; joy given is its harvest. Deal one delight today from stock received. (Stotra verse 95.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-095"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-890", title: "Naikajaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The repeatedly born, adventing again and again. Naikaja donns descents: births borrowed — dharma's crises summoning presence, the Endless (verse's opening) practising punctuation, eternity consenting to commas. Multiplicity of birth proves singularity of purpose: every advent serves the same rescue. Avatāra is not tourism but duty. Welcome one new beginning in your life as His small advent. (Stotra verse 95.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-095"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-891", title: "Agrajaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The first-born, eldest of all. Agraja holds primogeniture prime: senior supreme — the repeatedly-born (previous name) also the originally-born, first in every series He inaugurates, before whom all births queue. Firstness here is not privilege but responsibility: the Eldest answers first, carries most, forgives earliest. Every family, order and creation has Him as its eldest brother. Defer once to an elder — or act once as a worthy eldest. (Stotra verse 95.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-095"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-892", title: "Anirviṇṇaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The never-dejected (named again). Anirviṇṇa re-denies despair: gloom re-gloomlessly — cheer constitutional, the Eldest (previous name) never tired of the family's follies, patience as cosmology (the verse's teaching). Repetition is pastoral: dejection is the devotee's familiar visitor, so the hymn repeats the remedy. His unweariedness underwrites our recommencement. Re-deny one despair today on His authority. (Stotra verse 95.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-095"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-893", title: "Sadāmarṣī", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The ever-forbearing toward the good. Sadāmarṣin tolerates timelessly: forbearance forever — never-dejected (previous name) extended into active patience, enduring provocation without withdrawing protection. Forbearing differs from indulging: He waits without approving, bears without endorsing, giving the wanderer road while keeping the map. His patience is the world's foundation (next name). Forbear once towards a provoker today. (Stotra verse 95.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-095"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-894", title: "Lokādhiṣṭhānam", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The world's foundation, substratum of all. Lokādhiṣṭhāna founds well: cosmos carried commonly — forbearance (previous name) revealed as structural, patience as cosmology, the world standing because He stands under it. Theлока stands on tolerance the way a house stands on ground: remove the bearing and walls unlearn standing. Every world — of matter, of meaning, of your household — rests on Him. Mend one foundation quietly today. (Stotra verse 95.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-095"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-895", title: "Adbhutaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The Wonderful, wonder of wonders. Adbhuta apexes awe: amazement absolutized — the verse's arc from endlessness through eating, birth, patience and foundation ending not in system but in gasp. Philosophy peaks in wonder: after proof, breath, Self and freedom (elsewhere) or burden, birth and bearing (here), the honest response is marvel. Wonder is worship's mother tongue. Let one thing — ordinary, near — astonish you today. (Stotra verse 95.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-095"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-896", title: "Sanāt", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The Always-existing. Sanāt is ever-ness essential: always already — before ancient (next name superlatives it), the simple fact of eternal presence, existence without inauguration. The new verse opens with tense transcended: always, not since; being, not becoming. Before Kapila counts and blessing circulates, Is-ness stands. Rest once in always-ness beneath your calendar's urgencies. (Stotra verse 96.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-096"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-897", title: "Sanātanatamaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The most ancient of ancients. Sanātanatama superlatives seniority: oldness oldest — age apexed lest always-existing (previous name) sound abstract; eternity given wrinkles, venerability, the white beard of the cosmos. The superlative forbids rivals: every antiquity borrows, this one lends. Old here means original, not obsolete. Honour one old thing — custom, elder, text — as His portrait. (Stotra verse 96.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-096"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-898", title: "Kapilaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The sage Kapila, tawny light of enumeration. Kapila consecrates counting: enumeration enlightened — Sāṅkhya's founder recognized as His form, philosophy's categories as His tawny rays, reason itself an avatāra. After eternity comes wisdom: the Always-existing thinking itself out in twenty-five tattvas. Counting, done truly, is contemplation. Count one thing carefully today — breaths, blessings, truths — as Kapila would. (Stotra verse 96.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-096"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-899", title: "Kapiḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "Kapi — the sun who drinks the seas, the monkey who leapt the strait. Kapi joins thirst tremendous with leap luminous: the solar drinker exhausting oceans and Hanumān's Lord in one leap of a name — majesty and mischief, orb and ape, the grave and the playful. Wisdom (Kapila, previous name) now plays: enumeration's elder brother is leap. Both drink seas — one of thought, one of water. Leap once playfully over a strait you have been bridging heavily. (Stotra verse 96.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-096"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-900", title: "Avyayaḥ", category: "Bow-Sky", summary: "The undecaying (named again). Avyaya re-losslesses: waste re-wastelessed — eternity (verse's opening) sealing the sage-and-monkey pair, expenditure without loss, the Gītā's imperishable underwriting play and wisdom alike. Creation spends without depleting; blessing circulates (next names) without diminishing. The zone closes where banking began (great treasure, verse 86): all accounts balance in Him. Spend one kindness without counting. (Stotra verse 96.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-096"] },
]